70\t    # The overlay decides the NEXT session's permission mode and the\n271\t    # marker's prev_value is restored into it by night-mode off —\n272\t    # posture-write by proxy. No ceremony ever tool-edits these\n273\t    # (night-mode.py writes them as a process), so they sit at kernel\n274\t    # tier: no sentinel can grant a tool write. The WRITER script is\n275\t    # deliberately canonical-tier only (future ceremonies must be able\n276\t    # to evolve it under sentinel).\n277\t    \".claude/settings.local.json\",\n278\t    \".claude/state/night-mode.json\",\n279\t    # PLAN162_FIX_3 (PLAN-162 findings #3 + #8, consensus C10) — the two\n280\t    # files the guards themselves TRUST, previously guarded by NEITHER\n281\t    # rail (verified on HEAD: canonical=False, kernel=False for both):\n282\t    #\n283\t    #   1. the ADR-121 sentinel signer REGISTRY — the identity root\n284\t    #      `check_canonical_edit._sentinel_grants_path` consults on the\n285\t    #      dual signer rail. Unguarded, an ordinary Edit silently disarms\n286\t    #      half the signer check, asymmetric with the legacy\n287\t    #      `.claude/sentinel-signers.txt` which HAS been kernel-guarded\n288\t    #      since ADR-116.\n289\t    #   2. the policy hash-pin drift MANIFEST — it pins the policy files\n290\t    #      that are themselves guarded; leaving the pinner rewritable\n291\t    #      makes the pin decorative (\"pin the pinner\").\n292\t    #\n293\t    # Both are KERNEL tier (no sentinel escape) precisely because a\n294\t    # sentinel that grants an edit to the signer registry could authorize\n295\t    # its own future signer. Deliberately NOT paired with an\n296\t    # \"absence ⇒ fail-closed\" inversion (C10): that would need a\n297\t    # definition of \"expected\" which is itself editable, and would make\n298\t    # DELETING a file the way to choose the posture.\n299\t    \".claude/security/sentinel-signers-registry.yaml\",\n300\t    \".claude/policies/.drift-manifest.j